// Output. `writeByte(b)` writes the Byte b to the standard output and is b (a number written out where a Byte is wanted is that Byte: `writeByte(104)`), and a String has `print()`, which writes its bytes and
// is how many there are. A call that is made for what it does is a statement of its own and its result is not used (`greeting.print();`).
// What the program writes comes first, then the value it returns, on a line of its own, which `tersus run` and `tersus eval` print the same.

greeting = "Hello, ".concat("caf\u{e9}!\n");
count = greeting.print();
affirm count = size(greeting);

// One byte at a time: the letters of "ok", then a new line
writeByte(111);
writeByte(107);
writeByte(10);

return count;
